---
/**
 * website/src/components/CampaignHero.astro — the campaign hero (go-to-market plan, Task G2.2;
 * spec §6, section 1).
 *
 * **The two lines are the variant's, and this file types neither.** The headline is the `h1`
 * and the supporting line is the sentence under it, both read from `website/data/landing.yaml`
 * by the page and passed in here (G2.1, D102): the spec quotes three of the four headlines and
 * the data file writes them, so a campaign whose message changes changes the file and not this
 * component.
 *
 * **The first screen carries the action** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, finding 1). The
 * live preview's hero promised something, drew a small box beside the promise and put the only
 * way to act at the bottom of the page, so a visitor who had read the headline had nothing to
 * press. The booking link is now the hero's own third element, directly under the supporting
 * line and above the picture, at 1440x900 and at 390x844 — the consent bar and the header are
 * both above it in the flow and both counted. It is the same link the closing band offers
 * (`CampaignCalls.astro`), read from the same file, so the two are one booking and not two.
 *
 * **The headline is the page's, so its measure is the page's.** `.display-xl` is
 * `clamp(44px, 7.2vw, 104px)`, which is a size for a headline that owns the width of a band:
 * in the half-width column the hero used to give it, the longest variant's headline (48
 * characters) wrapped to five lines at 768 px and three at 1440 px. The owner's answer was to
 * widen the measure and cap the size, and the two are one rule below — the headline spans the
 * band and `clamp(17px, 5.2vw, 56px)` caps it. The size is read against the column at 390, 768
 * and 1440 px in `website/test/base-styles.mjs`, which is what keeps a later edit from putting
 * the fifth line back.
 *
 * **The hero's muted looping video — the spec's third element — is not here.** A variant's
 * `video` is a path under `website/public/` that the owner produces, no variant names one yet,
 * and the layout's `media` slot is where a picture or a video lands when one does (spec §6, F14,
 * G2.5). The hero therefore renders complete without it rather than with a hole where a file
 * would go.
 *
 * **What the eye lands on is a real screen of the console** (the owner's instruction of
 * 2026-10-30, after the design pass; D411): the variant's `screenshot`, a row of
 * `website/data/console-screens.yaml` that `website/data/landing.yaml` names by file — the Spend
 * screen for `/save`, whose subject is what a run is priced at, the run detail for `/control` —
 * the ledger, the verification and the findings a review policy's rounds end in — and the Live
 * screen for `/scale` and `/crew`, where the same console runs many at once. The picture is
 * shown large: it takes the hero's wider column on a wide viewport, where it is about half of a
 * 900 px first screen (a 16:10 capture in a column of the 1168 px band), and the full width of
 * the page under the booking action on a phone. It is framed the way the console page's slider
 * and the homepage's "See it" panel frame a screen — the panel behind it, the site's radius on
 * the picture and the screen's own caption under it — and everything about it but its size is
 * the data file's:
 * the `src` is derived from the row's name, and the `alt`, the caption and the two pixel
 * dimensions are the row's own, so nothing here writes a second copy of a sentence or a size
 * (F14, D190).
 *
 * **The picture is what the page fetches, and it is the page's one raster file.** It is the
 * hero's, so it is fetched with the page (`loading="eager"`) rather than when it comes near the
 * viewport, and it is the picture a reader on paid traffic came for, so it is asked for at
 * `fetchpriority="high"`. The `width` and `height` are the row's own pixels and are what
 * reserves the picture's box before its bytes land: the page does not move as it arrives.
 *
 * **The drawing is the fallback, and only a variant that names no screen renders it.**
 * `CampaignHeroArt.astro` draws the campaign's own picture — a task, the short-lived cloud job
 * it starts and the pull request the job ends in, painted from the variant's tone — and it is
 * what a variant with no `screenshot` shows in this slot. A variant that names one shows the
 * screen: one picture in the hero, and the two do not compete for it. Either way the picture is
 * rendered here rather than in the layout's `media` slot, because that slot sits *above* the
 * headline — a variant's own video belongs there, and a phone's first screen belongs to the
 * promise, the sentence that backs it and the booking action — so the picture follows the words
 * on a narrow viewport and sits beside them from the wide breakpoint, and it is suppressed
 * outright while a variant names media of its own, which takes that slot instead (F14, G2.5).
 *
 * **The figure carries `hero-shot` as well as the campaign's own class.** `hero-shot` is the
 * class the site's own lead readers take a hero's picture out of a page's opening by
 * (`website/test/budget.mjs`'s `heroIllustration` and `website/test/fold.mjs`'s rule 1 read it on
 * the homepage): a hero's picture is a picture rather than the page's opening prose, and the
 * campaign hero's is one. Without it the screen's alt text — which is the console's own and is
 * written for a reader who is not shown the picture — would be counted as the campaign's lead
 * (website/test/landing-pages.mjs holds the class there).
 *
 * **The tone rides on the section.** `data-tone` is the variant's own word from the file
 * (`playful` is `/crew`'s), written on the band beside the section's name and the file it was
 * read from; the drawing carries its own copy of it, which is what its accents and its four
 * motifs are keyed off, so the four pages are one template with one difference the data file
 * states and no page types a colour of its own.
 */
import CampaignHeroArt from './CampaignHeroArt.astro'
import CampaignIcon from './CampaignIcon.astro'
import type { Screen } from '../content.ts'

interface Props {
  /** The variant's headline, from `website/data/landing.yaml`: the page's one `h1`. */
  headline: string
  /** The variant's supporting line, the sentence under the headline. */
  supportingLine: string
  /** The variant's tone (`savings`, `control`, `scale`, `playful`). */
  tone: string
  /** The owner's booking link, from `website/data/landing.yaml`: the first screen's one action. */
  calendlyURL: string
  /**
   * Whether the hero draws the campaign's own picture. False while the variant names media of
   * its own, which takes the layout's `media` slot at the top of the page instead.
   */
  art: boolean
  /**
   * The console screen the hero shows, or null when the variant names no `screenshot` and the
   * drawing takes its place. A row of `website/data/console-screens.yaml`, so its `src`, its
   * `alt`, its caption and its two pixel dimensions are the data file's.
   */
  screenshot: Screen | null
}

const { headline, supportingLine, tone, calendlyURL, art, screenshot } = Astro.props
---

<section class="campaign-hero" data-section="hero" data-tone={tone} data-source="website/data/landing.yaml">
  {/* The words first in the markup, so the reading order is the order a phone shows and a
      screen reader hears: the promise, the sentence that backs it, the way to act on it, and
      then the picture. */}
  <h1 class="campaign-hero-headline display-xl">{headline}</h1>
  <div class="campaign-hero-body">
    <div class="campaign-hero-copy">
      <p class="campaign-hero-line">{supportingLine}</p>
      {/* The first screen's one action, and the same booking the closing band offers: the same
          link, and the same calendar mark beside the same words (`CampaignIcon.astro`), so the
          two actions a page offers are one control a reader meets twice rather than two
          buttons that happen to share a label. */}
      <p class="campaign-hero-action">
        <a class="campaign-hero-button" href={calendlyURL}><CampaignIcon name="calendar" />Book a demo</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    {/* One picture and no second one: the screen the variant named, or the drawing when it named
        none, or nothing while the variant carries media of its own in the slot above.
        `hero-shot` is beside the campaign's own class for the reason the header gives. */}
    {art &&
      (screenshot === null ? (
        <CampaignHeroArt tone={tone} />
      ) : (
        <figure class="campaign-hero-shot hero-shot">
          <img
            class="campaign-hero-shot-image"
            src={screenshot.src}
            alt={screenshot.alt}
            width={screenshot.width}
            height={screenshot.height}
            loading="eager"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption class="campaign-hero-shot-caption">{screenshot.caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): the type scale's steps, the site's ground and the tone the sentence
     under the headline is set in. */
  .campaign-hero {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-xl);
    padding-inline: var(--frame-gutter);
  }

  /* The headline takes the band, and its size is capped. The two are one rule and the arithmetic
     is `base-styles.mjs`'s: at 390 px the band's content box is 358 px and the longest headline
     is 48 characters, which is two lines up to 22.7 px and three at the 44 px `.display-xl`
     floors at. 5.5 vw is under that ceiling at every width — the ceiling *rises* as a share of
     the viewport (22.7/390, 45.8/768) because the gutter is a smaller part of a wider page — and
     `--font-size-4xl` is the ceiling a wide viewport stops at: 56 px at 1440, where the band is
     1168 px wide (the wide breakpoint's gutter is `max(24px, (1440px − 1216px)/2 + 24px)`, 136
     px, and `base.css` redefines `--frame-gutter` with `--space-md` from 48 rem for exactly
     that). The floor is `--font-size-lg` rather than the body's step, so the page's `h1` never
     falls below the type around it, and `base-styles.mjs` holds both ends: the line count at
     the three widths and the headline's lead over every other heading on the page.

     The 5.5 vw is the largest slope the two-line rule allows at 390 px. That is the whole
     tension of this rule and it is worth naming: a 48-character headline on two lines in 358 px
     is a ~21 px headline, so on a phone the `h1` is *smaller* than a section heading would be at
     `.display-lg`'s 32 px floor. The page answers it by scaling its own section type with the
     viewport too (`CampaignSteps.astro`, `CampaignCalls.astro`, both under this slope), so the
     `h1` is the largest type on the page at all three measured widths rather than only from
     615 px up. The alternative — a 32 px phone `h1` — is three lines for three of the four
     headlines, which is the wrap the owner asked to be rid of. */
  .campaign-hero-headline {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-lg), 5.5vw, var(--font-size-4xl));
  }

  /* The sentence under the headline is the body's size on a phone and the body's larger step
     from the wide breakpoint: at 390 px a 21 px headline over a 20 px sentence is not a
     headline and its lead, and the pair is one of the things the case in `base-styles.mjs`
     reads. */
  .campaign-hero-line {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .campaign-hero-action {
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 0;
  }

  /* The first screen's one action: the accent as its fill and the ground as its label, the pair
     `a11y.mjs` computes for a filled button, and the shape the homepage's own button is — a
     56 px control a thumb finds, at the width of its column. It is a link and not a button
     element because the booking is a page the visitor goes to. */
  .campaign-hero-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* The calendar mark and the label are one row, spaced by the layout rather than by a margin
       on the icon — the same pair the closing band's booking is. */
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 56px;
    padding: 0 var(--space-xl);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .campaign-hero-button:active {
    transform: scale(0.97);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .campaign-hero-button:hover {
      background: var(--accent-on-dark);
    }
  }

  /* The hero's picture: the console screen the variant names, in the frame the console page's
     slider and the homepage's "See it" panel give a screen — the panel one step above the
     ground, the site's radius, and the screen's own caption under the picture at its right
     edge — so a reader who has met the screens there meets them the same way here. The
     figure's own margin is reset:
     a `<figure>` arrives with one, and the hero's stack is spaced by its rules rather than by
     the user agent's. There is no width rule here because the picture needs none: on a phone the
     hero is a plain stack and the figure takes the content box, and from the wide breakpoint it
     is the second grid column, where it is the wider of the two (3fr of the band's 1168 px on a
     1440 px viewport, and a 16:10 capture is then about 420 px of a 900 px first screen, which
     is the half the owner asked for). */
  .campaign-hero-shot {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--panel);
  }

  /* The picture takes its box and its own ratio, which the row's `width` and `height` reserve
     before the file lands (base.css caps an image at its container's width and keeps its
     ratio). The hairline step of the surface behind it is what separates a screen whose own
     background is the page's colour from the page. */
  .campaign-hero-shot-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  /* The line under the picture is the data file's, and its box is its own words' width at the
     picture's right edge, the way the slider sets the same sentence. */
  .campaign-hero-shot-caption {
    width: fit-content;
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0 auto;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  /* A phone's frame takes less of the picture's width: at 390 px the content box is 358 px and
     the frame's own padding is a visible share of the screen a reader came to see. */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .campaign-hero-shot {
      padding: var(--space-2xs);
    }
  }

  /* The entrance (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, finding 5). The headline's own rise is
     `base.css`'s — `main h1.display-xl:not(.hero-tagline)` is every inner page's header, and a
     campaign hero is one — so what is written here is the rest of the stagger: the sentence
     100 ms after it, the button 200 ms after, and the picture 300 ms after that, each rising
     the same 18 px over the site's own entrance duration. CSS only, on the first frame the
     browser draws: a page with no script, or a browser without `@starting-style`, shows the
     hero as it is, and a reader who asked their system for less motion never gets the block at
     all rather than getting it neutralised. The button's own feedback — its hover and its
     press — is declared here too and for the same reason: the campaign's motion is written for
     a reader who has not asked for less, so nothing on these four pages leans on a rule
     elsewhere to undo it (`website/test/base-styles.mjs` reads that, and `base.css`'s universal
     reduce rule is the second half of the pair for everything the site does not gate).

     **The picture is the screen here rather than the drawing, and the two are one class.** The
     drawing carries the same 300 ms entrance in its own file, and that rule is the one that runs
     on a page whose variant names no screen. On the pages as shipped the hero's picture is the
     console screen, so the element that rises is this class's, and the drawing's rule is for an
     element the page does not render. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .campaign-hero-button {
      transition:
        background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    /* The calendar mark travels with the plate it sits on, the 2 px lift and nudge the closing
       band's booking answers a pointer with: the first screen's action and the last one are the
       same booking, so they answer the same way (`CampaignCalls.astro`). */
    .campaign-hero-button :global(.campaign-icon) {
      transition: transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    /* `:not(:active)` for the same reason the closing band's rules carry it: the press below is
       this rule's sibling at the same specificity, and a mouse press is `:hover` and `:active` at
       once — without the exclusion the lift would answer it and the press would read as nothing
       (`CampaignCalls.astro`). */
    @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
      .campaign-hero-button:hover:not(:active) {
        transform: translateY(-2px);
      }

      .campaign-hero-button:hover:not(:active) :global(.campaign-icon[data-icon='calendar']) {
        transform: translateY(-2px);
      }
    }

    .campaign-hero-line,
    .campaign-hero-action,
    .campaign-hero-shot {
      transition:
        opacity var(--duration-entrance) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--duration-entrance) var(--ease-out);
    }

    .campaign-hero-line {
      transition-delay: 100ms;
    }

    .campaign-hero-action {
      transition-delay: 200ms;
    }

    .campaign-hero-shot {
      transition-delay: 300ms;
    }

    @starting-style {
      .campaign-hero-line,
      .campaign-hero-action,
      .campaign-hero-shot {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(18px);
      }
    }
  }

  /* The wide viewport is where the hero becomes two columns: the sentence and the action on the
     reading start and the picture on the outer edge, level with each other. The headline is
     above both, which is what lets it be the width of the band at every width — the measure the
     owner asked for — and it is the reason the picture can be the biggest thing in the hero
     without the action being under it. The two-column rule is inside the breakpoint and nowhere
     else, so the phone's hero is the plain stack the markup's order gives it. */
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .campaign-hero {
      padding-block: var(--space-3xl) var(--space-2xl);
    }

    .campaign-hero-body {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
      align-items: end;
      column-gap: var(--space-xl);
    }

    /* The copy keeps the first column and the picture the second, whether it is a screen or the
       drawing: a variant with media of its own is the same hero with one column painted. */
    .campaign-hero-copy {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    /* The sentence takes the body's larger step here, where the headline is 42 px and up: it is
       the pair the case in `base-styles.mjs` reads, and the headline's lead over it only widens
       with the viewport. */
    .campaign-hero-line {
      font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    }
  }
</style>
